<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Leclerc (general)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles Victoire Emmanuel Leclerc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Victoire Emmanuel Leclerc was a French general and the husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister Pauline Bonaparte. In 1801–1802 he led the large expedition sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> to restore French authority; he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> by treachery and deported him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but the attempt to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> provoked renewed revolt. Leclerc died of yellow fever in November 1802, and his army was defeated in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-leclerc-general.jpg" alt="Charles Leclerc (general)" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Kinson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9n%C3%A9ral_CHARLES-EMMANUEL_LECLERC_(1772-1802).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
